      {
       "text": "A product roadmap is intended to be a strategic compass, aligning teams around a shared vision and guiding them toward meaningful business objectives. Yet, in practice, most roadmaps fail. They become obsolete shortly after being drafted, foster resentment between engineers and business stakeholders, and produce features that fail to move the needle. This systemic failure stems from a fundamental misunderstanding of what a roadmap should be: too often, it substitutes outputs for outcomes, mistakes planning for certainty, and prioritizes rigid commitments over continuous learning.\n\nThe primary reason roadmaps fail is the illusion of certainty. To satisfy executives, sales teams, or investors, product managers frequently design roadmaps as feature-laden Gantt charts mapped to quarterly deadlines. This treats modern software development as predictable manufacturing rather than iterative discovery. In reality, user preferences, technical complexities, and market dynamics change constantly. When a twelve-month plan is treated as a locked contract, teams are forced to choose between cutting corners to hit arbitrary dates or taking the blame for \"failing\" to deliver on time.\n\nClosely related is the fixation on outputs over outcomes. The vast majority of failed roadmaps are merely prioritized feature wishlists. They document *what* will be built rather than *why* it matters or *what problem* it solves. When success is measured by velocity and shipping dates rather than business impact or customer satisfaction, the roadmap loses its purpose. Teams celebrate delivering a complex feature on time, completely ignoring the fact that it failed to drive adoption, retention, or revenue. \n\nAdditionally, roadmaps frequently fail because they function as political compromises rather than strategic choices. In an attempt to placate every internal stakeholder—from sales and marketing to executive leadership—roadmaps become bloated grab-bags of disconnected initiatives. Strategy requires saying no; a roadmap that attempts to please everyone dilutes focus and exhausts engineering resources. Worse, this political buy-in makes the roadmap resistant to change. When new data surfaces, teams are often discouraged from pivoting because a pivot is viewed as an admission of poor planning rather than an intelligent response to reality.\n\nTo be effective, the product roadmap must be reimagined not as an unyielding delivery schedule, but as a living hypothesis. Organizations must abandon granular, long-term feature commitments and adopt outcome-based frameworks, such as the \"Now, Next, Later\" model, which prioritize customer problems over predetermined solutions. Until teams accept that uncertainty is an inherent feature of innovation, roadmaps will remain expensive, demoralizing exercises in collective wishful thinking.",
